# NET G cell — the STAGE-1-ONLY class. set arm.
#
# AXIS CELL: comp-kind = set `{...}`  ·  source = String (codepoints)  ·
# destination = `auto` (the EMPTY result channel)  ·  FILTERED.
#
# ⚠ THIS CELL EXISTS BECAUSE NO ORDINARY GATE CAN SEE ITS CLASS. Every
# self-host gate in the tree runs the STAGE-0 driver (the one Rust gg built),
# and `self_host_bootstrap_fixed_point` compares emitted C *text*, not
# behaviour. The filter travels through function PARAMETERS as a boxed
# option; at HEAD that boxed form is not dereferenced at the dispatch
# boundary, so EVERY FILTERED comprehension is wrong once the compiler is
# compiled by itself. The fix flattens the parameter at the dispatch
# boundary, which is what takes stage 1 from broken to correct.
#
# ⚠ DO NOT "TIDY" THE FLATTENED PARAMETER BACK INTO THE BOXED SHAPE. The
# elegance-showcase rule actively invites that cleanup and it is the one
# change this cell exists to catch. The wired test
# `self_host_comprehension_stage1_net` builds a STAGE-1 binary and runs this
# cell through it — that is the only instrument that sees the class.
#
# The `.len()` assertion is KEPT beside the element read on purpose: for this
# class the LENGTH is the observable (a dropped filter changes the count),
# while the element read is what catches a mint defect.
#
# RED-VERIFIED: stage 0 at HEAD prints `0` where `2` is correct (the §4 silent drop); stage 1 at HEAD is wrong too
#
# FIXTURE PLACEMENT: subdirectory (see any NET A cell's header).
# LANE: self-host only.  STAGE: 0 AND 1.  RUNG: 1a (both real lanes agree).

String mk(String a, String b):
    return a + b

void main():
    String s0 = mk("ab", "c")
    auto v = {c for c in s0 if c != mk("b", "")}
    print(v.len())
    print(v.contains(mk("a", "")))
    print(v.contains(mk("c", "")))
